+# In-Application Specialty Filtering for the Veterinarian Directory
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`REQ-VET-003` filters both veterinarian-directory surfaces by a specialty, matched against the whole specialty name and disregarding letter case. The page-at-a-time surface must page over the filtered set, and a blank filter must behave as no filter.
+
+The veterinarian collection is small, standalone, and already fully cached under the `vets` cache. The comparable filter elsewhere — owner search — pushes its match to the database through a Spring Data derived query. That path carries a known per-vendor defect: PostgreSQL stores the last name case-sensitively, so the same case-insensitive search returns different results per database (see [system-design.md#known-defects](../system-design.md#known-defects) and `REQ-OWN-002`). A database-level case-insensitive specialty match would inherit the same divergence, because the three vendor schemas reach case-insensitivity three different ways.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Repository-derived query** — add a `findDistinctBySpecialties_NameIgnoreCase` method returning a paged/collection result. Filters and pages at the database, mirroring owner search. Case-insensitivity depends on each vendor's collation, and a parameterized cached method multiplies cache keys.
+2. **In-application filter over the cached collection** — read the full cached collection via the existing `findAll()`, match whole specialty name with `equalsIgnoreCase` in Java, and page the filtered list in the controller. The unfiltered path stays exactly as today.
+
+## Decision
+
+We filter in the application (Option 2). When a non-blank specialty is supplied, the controller reads the cached veterinarian collection, keeps those holding a specialty whose whole name matches case-insensitively, and constructs the page from the filtered list. A blank or whitespace-only value is stripped and treated as no filter, exactly as owner search treats an empty last name. With no filter, each surface uses its existing unfiltered path unchanged.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Case-insensitive matching is vendor-independent: it runs in the JVM, so it cannot reproduce the PostgreSQL case-sensitivity defect that owner search carries.
+- The existing `vets` cache is reused as-is; no parameterized cached query is added, so no new cache keys are created.
+- Paging over a filtered set is computed in-application, diverging from the owner-search push-to-database precedent. The divergence is deliberate and documented here so a reader does not read it as an oversight.
+- The approach relies on the veterinarian collection staying small enough to filter in memory. This holds for a standalone directory; a large veterinarian population would force a reassessment.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md#contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) — the `VetController` contract realizing this decision
+- [system-design.md#known-defects](../system-design.md#known-defects) — the PostgreSQL owner-search case-sensitivity defect this decision avoids reproducing
